# Pinned third-party payload pieces.
#
# These are NOT vendored in the repo: bin/fetch-assets.sh downloads them into
# vendor/ (gitignored) and refuses anything whose sha256 does not match. Bump
# a version here and the next build picks it up; the old file stays cached.
#
# To bump:  edit the version -- for HelixScreen that means HELIX_FILE too,
#           which embeds it, and for the PYPKG_* python packages the FILE is
#           the only place the version appears -- set the matching sha256 to
#           SKIP, then run
#           `make vendor`. It prints the real hash to paste back over SKIP.
#           With a stale hash left in place fetch-assets.sh exits 1 instead of
#           printing anything.

MAINSAIL_VERSION="v2.18.2"
MAINSAIL_SHA256="df2ba7c301f7bfc8ac9f122741a6ba08356d679ecfa1f62f898d0337802d5de5"

# Klipper: the creator5 branch of Klipper4FlashForge/klipper -- upstream
# v0.13.0 plus the FlashForge commits the printer actually needs (see the
# fork's README-creator5.md). A COMMIT, because the branch moves; bump it by
# pasting the new head sha here and re-hashing.
#
# This pin is what a release ships. KLIPPER_FORK in config.env still points
# the build at a local checkout instead -- for working on the fork itself --
# but when it is unset, bin/patch.sh extracts this tarball and builds
# c_helper.so from ITS sources with the pinned toolchain below, so the .so
# can never be older than the klippy tree beside it. The v20260824 release
# shipped without any of this: KLIPPER_FORK was empty in CI, patch.sh
# silently kept the stock 0.12-era tree, and the package half-overwrote the
# fork on updated printers -- klippy died at connect with a cffi arg-count
# error. patch.sh now refuses to build a fork package without a fork.
KLIPPER_VERSION="b722a4c8b1aedfb939a7688a9b30e7864fddd505"
KLIPPER_SHA256="dbd81ad75701a56562708a398be9efda1cfa9af84211445e5c86f0683c2326e4"

# The cross-toolchain that builds c_helper.so: Ingenic's gcc 7.2.0 /
# glibc 2.29 for the X2000 (mips32r2, little-endian, o32, nan2008 -- the
# printer's kernel refuses anything else, and Debian's mips cross-compilers
# produce big-endian or legacy-nan objects). The same toolchain the K1
# community uses for the identical SoC family; mirrored as a release asset
# by ballaswag/k1-discovery. ~203MB, so fetch-assets.sh only pulls it when
# the build actually needs to compile chelper.
MIPS_TOOLCHAIN_VERSION="1.0.0"
MIPS_TOOLCHAIN_FILE="mips-gcc720-glibc229.tar.gz"
MIPS_TOOLCHAIN_SHA256="c0415a857c6b384820ec61b8a30c89e528585f3ef07d580fc856e80313c9d2e8"

HELIX_VERSION="v0.99.115-creator5.1"
HELIX_FILE="helixscreen-creator5-v0.99.115.tar.gz"
HELIX_SHA256="7205738ca8ccb0aa9a95262506a87283712ab5544ce4abdbcbee571f51395a61"

# Moonraker. The stock one is a 2022 build (API 1.0.5) that predates the
# webcam "enabled" flag that modern Mainsail requires -- see bin/patch.sh.
#
# A COMMIT, NOT A RELEASE, and not a matter of taste. FlashForge built python
# 3.8.2 WITHOUT the _sqlite3 module and shipped no libsqlite3 anywhere on the
# printer, so every Moonraker from v0.9.0 onward dies at startup with
# "ModuleNotFoundError: No module named '_sqlite3'" -- v0.9.0 is where the
# database moved from lmdb to sqlite. The last release that still uses lmdb is
# v0.8.0, and that one predates the webcam flag. No release has both, so this
# pins the newest commit that has the webcam fix and still uses lmdb: 9d0d09d,
# 2023-12-05, the commit before 80c7620 "database: migrate from lmdb to
# sqlite". lmdb 1.3.0 is installed, so the existing database is read in place
# and nothing is converted.
#
# Before moving this pin: the blocker is native modules, not the python
# version (Moonraker still supports >=3.7). Check every dependency of a newer
# build against what is actually in Python-3.8.2/lib/python3.8/lib-dynload and
# site-packages on the factory image -- a missing C extension is fatal and a
# pure-python one is not.
#
# CHECK IT BEFORE YOU SHIP IT. Nothing on the printer checks this any more and
# there is no stock build to fall back to: the installer replaces Moonraker
# unconditionally, so a pin the interpreter cannot load leaves the machine
# without a web UI until the next flash. `make test-moonraker` is where that is
# caught -- it imports every configured component on the printer's own
# interpreter, in the replica, before anything ships.
MOONRAKER_VERSION="9d0d09de8063922696359c1b88c86a86d6fdb296"
MOONRAKER_SHA256="6ddcba08a1a41f173fc6ee393a24e41f27b25f079e7c49921b87273ecff595a8"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- s6
# The process supervisor. skalibs is s6's own C library and is a BUILD
# dependency only -- its headers and .a are used to link s6 and are never
# shipped -- so the two are pinned together and bumped together: skarnet
# releases them as a matched set and s6 will not build against a skalibs from
# a different generation. execline is deliberately absent: s6 `run` scripts
# are plain `#!/bin/sh`, and execline would be another 53 binaries / 2.1MB
# for nothing.
#
# THREE things about this build are load-bearing, all of them measured on the
# replica rather than reasoned about (see tools/supervisor/README.md):
#
#   * It is configured --prefix=/usr/data/anvil, because s6 bakes its
#     libexecdir in AT COMPILE TIME: every waiting verb (s6-svc -w,
#     s6-svwait) execs s6-svlisten, which spawns s6-ftrigrd from that baked-in
#     path. Built for any other prefix, `s6-svstat` and respawn keep working
#     and only the waiting verbs fail -- so it fails late and partially, which
#     is the worst way for a supervisor to fail. Moving $MODDIR means
#     rebuilding s6.
#   * It is compiled with -D_FILE_OFFSET_BITS=64. Without it readdir()
#     returns EOVERFLOW on this box (32-bit build, 64-bit inodes) and
#     s6-svscan cannot see its own service directory: it starts cleanly and
#     then supervises nothing.
#   * It is linked against musl, statically. Static glibc produced a 73MB
#     tree here; musl produces 3.6MB, of which we ship ~930KB.
SKALIBS_VERSION="2.15.1.0"
SKALIBS_SHA256="f9c905e74935c6fe911c7e344e3e89d5fbd2014c1a04650b524b15ce9b5635d1"
S6_VERSION="2.15.1.0"
S6_SHA256="eab9c46e22b66b16135f9a05ec68a0ea287d9060b84d10defaaa2caad158ab52"

# The musl cross-toolchain that builds s6: mipsel-buildroot-linux-musl, from
# Bootlin's toolchain project (https://toolchains.bootlin.com), NOT musl.cc.
# NOT the Ingenic toolchain above either -- that one is glibc, and glibc is
# what makes a static s6 73MB. Two toolchains is not duplication: c_helper.so
# is a shared object dlopen'd by the printer's own glibc python and must
# match it, while s6 is a set of standalone static binaries that must match
# nothing.
#
# WHY BOOTLIN AND NOT musl.cc, WHICH THIS WAS PINNED TO UNTIL NOW. musl.cc is
# a single volunteer-run host with no CDN behind it, and it went unreachable
# from CI -- and briefly from a developer machine too -- for long enough,
# more than once, to make a build unable to fetch a toolchain it had fetched
# fine the previous run. Bootlin publishes prebuilt Buildroot toolchains for
# a wide range of targets as an ongoing, funded project with real hosting
# behind it; this is target mips32el, variant musl, and the file below is
# real quantity: cross-compiled skalibs+s6 with it, stripped, ran under
# qemu-mipsel-static AND on the printer replica, correctly ELF32 LE MIPS
# (e_flags carries the same base 0x1007 -- mips1, legacy NaN -- as the
# musl.cc build, plus PIC/CPIC bits from musl's own -fPIC libc.a, which cost
# nothing: a fully static binary that never dlopens anything does not care).
#
# UNLIKE musl.cc's tarball, THIS ONE IS VERSIONED and its own archive
# extracts into a directory named after that version, not a fixed
# "$S6_HOST-cross" -- bin/patch.sh renames it after extraction so the rest of
# the build does not have to know the version string. Bump
# MUSL_TOOLCHAIN_FILE/_SHA256 together from a new release's own .sha256 file
# at the URL fetch-assets.sh uses; re-run the case-supervisor gate before
# pasting a new value here.
#
# ~70MB (smaller than musl.cc's ~100MB -- newer musl, more aggressive
# stripping upstream), so fetch-assets.sh only pulls it when the s6 build
# actually needs to compile -- work/.s6 caches the built tree between runs.
MUSL_TOOLCHAIN_FILE="mips32el--musl--stable-2025.08-1.tar.xz"
MUSL_TOOLCHAIN_SHA256="12a5e2f50b94b5c619730914db380ce9679798fb3c7460a40d480f77bb50628f"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- CPython
# A SECOND Python for the printer, cross-compiled here and SHIPPED BUT NOT
# USED (bin/patch.sh section 5c). FlashForge's own /usr/prog/Python-3.8.2 is
# still what everything runs on -- see payload/anvil-env.sh.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS AT ALL. FlashForge built 3.8.2 without _sqlite3 and shipped no
# libsqlite3 anywhere on the rootfs, which is the single fact that pins
# MOONRAKER_VERSION above to a 2023 commit: every Moonraker from v0.9.0 on
# stores its database in sqlite and dies at startup on this interpreter. An
# interpreter that can `import sqlite3` is what unpins that, and it is 3.13
# rather than a 3.8 with one module added because a from-source build costs
# the same either way. Measured on the replica: the stock 3.8.2 cannot import
# sqlite3 and this one creates a database, writes it, closes it and reads it
# back. test/integration/printer/case-python.sh is that measurement, kept.
#
# EVERY VERSION BELOW IS PINNED WITH A sha256 FOR THE SAME REASON THE REST OF
# THIS FILE IS: nothing binary is vendored, so the only thing standing between
# a source URL and code the printer executes is the hash. python.org, sqlite.org
# and skarnet are not equally trustworthy, but they are equally irrelevant once
# the hash is checked.
#
# What each pin is load-bearing for, because "bump to latest" is wrong for at
# least four of them:
#
#   PY_VERSION       The interpreter, and the ONLY version this build works
#                    for as written: cross-building CPython needs a build
#                    python of the SAME version (configure hard-errors
#                    otherwise), so patch.sh builds an x86-64 3.13.7 from this
#                    same tarball first. Bump it and both halves move
#                    together, which is why there is one variable.
#   OPENSSL_VERSION  3.0.x DELIBERATELY, not 3.1/3.2/3.5. Two things about
#                    3.0.15 are relied on and both were hit for real: `no-docs`
#                    does not exist before 3.1 (patch.sh must not pass it), and
#                    the linux-mips32 target bakes `-mips2` into its cflags,
#                    which this toolchain -- default -mfp64 -- rejects unless
#                    an explicit -mips32r2 lands after it on the command line.
#                    3.0 is also the LTS branch (security fixes to 2026-09).
#                    Moving to 3.5 means re-deriving the target flags.
#   SQLITE_*         THE reason the whole build exists. The amalgamation
#                    ("autoconf") tarball, not the source tree: one .c file, no
#                    tcl, no fossil. Note the URL carries a YEAR directory that
#                    does not appear in the version number, hence SQLITE_YEAR.
#   ZLIB_VERSION     zlib.net serves current releases from /fossils/ once they
#                    are superseded, so the URL below is the stable one for an
#                    old version as well as a current one. Needed by the zlib
#                    module and by OpenSSL's compression.
#   LIBFFI_VERSION   _ctypes. Nothing on the rootfs can stand in: it ships
#                    libffi.so.7 and FlashForge's own /usr/prog carries .so.8,
#                    which is exactly the mess a static libffi.a avoids.
#   XZ_VERSION       lzma. 5.4.7 and not 5.6.x ON PURPOSE: 5.6.0/5.6.1 are the
#                    backdoored releases (CVE-2024-3094). 5.4.7 is the
#                    maintained branch that never carried it.
#   BZIP2_VERSION    bz2. Last release was 2019; this is not a stale pin, it is
#                    the whole project.
#   EXPAT_*          pyexpat, and with it every stdlib XML parser. Built here
#                    rather than left to CPython's bundled copy so there is one
#                    expat to patch when the next parser CVE lands. The tag has
#                    its own spelling (R_2_6_4) because libexpat's git tags use
#                    underscores; both appear in the URL.
#
# IF ONE OF THESE MOVES AND THE HASH IS NOT UPDATED, fetch-assets.sh stops the
# build. If a hash is updated without re-running the gate, the failure is
# silent and lands on a printer: a dependency that quietly stops linking makes
# CPython record the module as "missing" rather than fail, which is precisely
# how _sqlite3 disappeared during the spike. `make test-python` is the check.
PY_VERSION="3.13.7"
PY_SHA256="6c9d80839cfa20024f34d9a6dd31ae2a9cd97ff5e980e969209746037a5153b2"
OPENSSL_VERSION="3.0.15"
OPENSSL_SHA256="23c666d0edf20f14249b3d8f0368acaee9ab585b09e1de82107c66e1f3ec9533"
SQLITE_VERSION="3460100"
SQLITE_YEAR="2024"
SQLITE_SHA256="67d3fe6d268e6eaddcae3727fce58fcc8e9c53869bdd07a0c61e38ddf2965071"
ZLIB_VERSION="1.3.1"
ZLIB_SHA256="9a93b2b7dfdac77ceba5a558a580e74667dd6fede4585b91eefb60f03b72df23"
LIBFFI_VERSION="3.4.6"
LIBFFI_SHA256="b0dea9df23c863a7a50e825440f3ebffabd65df1497108e5d437747843895a4e"
XZ_VERSION="5.4.7"
XZ_SHA256="8db6664c48ca07908b92baedcfe7f3ba23f49ef2476864518ab5db6723836e71"
BZIP2_VERSION="1.0.8"
BZIP2_SHA256="ab5a03176ee106d3f0fa90e381da478ddae405918153cca248e682cd0c4a2269"
EXPAT_VERSION="2.6.4"
EXPAT_TAG="R_2_6_4"
EXPAT_SHA256="fd03b7172b3bd7427a3e7a812063f74754f24542429b634e0db6511b53fb2278"

# ------------------------------------------- CPython's third-party packages
# The 18 sdists that become $MODDIR/lib/python3.13/site-packages -- what
# Moonraker and klippy import, cross-built for mipsel by bin/patch.sh section
# 5c against the interpreter pinned above. SHIPPED BUT NOT USED, exactly like
# the interpreter itself: payload/anvil-env.sh still points FF_PYTHON at
# FlashForge's 3.8.2 and the switch is a later commit.
#
# THE LIST IS NOT moonraker-requirements.txt, and that is a decision rather
# than an omission. That file installs every OPTIONAL component's
# dependencies -- apprise, ldap3, paho-mqtt, zeroconf, python-periphery. None
# of those components is enabled in assets/moonraker.conf, so none of them is
# built. What is here is the closure of what the ENABLED set imports
# (moonraker.conf's sections plus server.CORE_COMPONENTS) plus klippy's, and
# nothing else. msgspec and uvloop are absent on purpose: Moonraker guards
# both with contextlib.suppress(ImportError) and falls back to the stdlib,
# which was measured rather than assumed.
#
# Every one is an SDIST, and bin/patch.sh builds them with `--no-binary :all:`
# for a reason that is recorded here because it cost real time to find: pip
# will HELPFULLY download a prebuilt manylinux wheel when one exists, and on
# the first run of the spike it did exactly that for cffi, greenlet and
# tornado. Three x86-64 .so files went into the tree, built cleanly, imported
# cleanly on the build host, and would have failed at import on the printer
# with nothing but ENOEXEC to explain themselves. Only the ABI gate caught
# them. A wheel is not a build; it is somebody else's build.
#
# WHERE THE URLS COME FROM. bin/fetch-assets.sh composes
# files.pythonhosted.org/packages/source/<initial>/<project>/<file>, the
# stable path form, rather than the hashed /packages/<a>/<b>/<64 hex>/ one a
# browser copies out of PyPI -- that path embeds a digest of the file and
# changes shape with no version bump, which is a URL nobody can maintain by
# hand. So the FILE is what is pinned here: it carries the version, the way
# HELIX_FILE does, and its SPELLING is upstream's business (MarkupSafe,
# dbus_next and streaming_form_data are not the names their projects use).
#
# PYPKG_LIST is the build order and the ship list at once; bin/patch.sh
# iterates it and bin/fetch-assets.sh fetches it, so a package added here and
# nowhere else is fetched, built, gated and shipped. The variable names are
# the list entry uppercased with - as _, which is how both scripts find them.
PYPKG_LIST="distro inotify-simple libnacl dbus-next preprocess-cancellation
            pyserial pyserial-asyncio pycparser smart-open jinja2 setuptools
            tornado markupsafe cffi greenlet lmdb streaming-form-data pillow"

# ---- pure python. No compiler is invoked; they are here to be PRESENT. ----
PYPKG_DISTRO_FILE="distro-1.9.0.tar.gz"
PYPKG_DISTRO_SHA256="2fa77c6fd8940f116ee1d6b94a2f90b13b5ea8d019b98bc8bafdcabcdd9bdbed"
PYPKG_INOTIFY_SIMPLE_FILE="inotify_simple-1.3.5.tar.gz"
PYPKG_INOTIFY_SIMPLE_SHA256="8440ffe49c4ae81a8df57c1ae1eb4b6bfa7acb830099bfb3e305b383005cc128"
# libnacl is pure python and reaches libsodium through ctypes -- which is why
# SODIUM_VERSION at the bottom of this file exists at all, and why a bump to
# either one is a reason to re-run the ed25519 half of the moonraker gate.
PYPKG_LIBNACL_FILE="libnacl-2.1.0.tar.gz"
PYPKG_LIBNACL_SHA256="f3418da7df29e6d9b11fd7d990289d16397dc1020e4e35192e11aee826922860"
PYPKG_DBUS_NEXT_FILE="dbus_next-0.2.3.tar.gz"
PYPKG_DBUS_NEXT_SHA256="f4eae26909332ada528c0a3549dda8d4f088f9b365153952a408e28023a626a5"
# preprocess-cancellation 0.2.1 is a poetry project whose sdist declares NO
# [build-system] table at all and ships no setup.py, so there is no backend to
# call and pip's legacy fallback would have to guess. It is also ONE FILE, so
# bin/patch.sh copies preprocess_cancellation.py into site-packages rather
# than building it -- see the comment there. (The same release also declares
# version 0.0.0 in its own metadata, a broken upstream release, so a version
# specifier would be refused by pip even if there were something to build.)
PYPKG_PREPROCESS_CANCELLATION_FILE="preprocess_cancellation-0.2.1.tar.gz"
PYPKG_PREPROCESS_CANCELLATION_SHA256="e2f1224e1ba1603bdfdbf6937caaf91082dc849e5122e80a5328aa999433ce79"
PYPKG_PYSERIAL_FILE="pyserial-3.4.tar.gz"
PYPKG_PYSERIAL_SHA256="6e2d401fdee0eab996cf734e67773a0143b932772ca8b42451440cfed942c627"
PYPKG_PYSERIAL_ASYNCIO_FILE="pyserial-asyncio-0.6.tar.gz"
PYPKG_PYSERIAL_ASYNCIO_SHA256="b6032923e05e9d75ec17a5af9a98429c46d2839adfaf80604d52e0faacd7a32f"
PYPKG_PYCPARSER_FILE="pycparser-2.22.tar.gz"          # cffi's only runtime dep
PYPKG_PYCPARSER_SHA256="491c8be9c040f5390f5bf44a5b07752bd07f56edf992381b05c701439eec10f6"
# smart_open is not optional, whatever the name suggests, and it is not
# Moonraker that wants it: streaming-form-data's targets.py imports it at
# MODULE SCOPE, so `import streaming_form_data` raises ModuleNotFoundError
# without it. Measured in the replica, where every other package imported and
# that one did not. moonraker-requirements.txt pins it for the same reason.
PYPKG_SMART_OPEN_FILE="smart_open-6.4.0.tar.gz"
PYPKG_SMART_OPEN_SHA256="be3c92c246fbe80ebce8fbacb180494a481a77fcdcb7c1aadb2ea5b9c2bee8b9"
PYPKG_JINJA2_FILE="jinja2-3.1.4.tar.gz"
PYPKG_JINJA2_SHA256="4a3aee7acbbe7303aede8e9648d13b8bf88a429282aa6122a993f0ac800cb369"
# setuptools/pkg_resources are a runtime dependency of nothing in this list.
# They are the fix for a MEASURED failure: a tree without them made lmdb fall
# back to its cffi path and try to invoke mips-linux-gnu-gcc ON THE PRINTER at
# Moonraker startup, and there is no compiler on a printer. (Installing lmdb
# as a wheel rather than as an egg removes that path as well, so this is now
# belt and braces -- 4.9MB of it, 36% of site-packages, and the one obvious
# thing to drop if the data partition ever gets tight.)
#
# It is ALSO the host-side build backend for most of this list: patch.sh
# installs this same sdist into the throwaway x86-64 build-python. One pin,
# both jobs -- so the setuptools that generates the shipped .so files is the
# setuptools whose hash is checked here.
PYPKG_SETUPTOOLS_FILE="setuptools-75.6.0.tar.gz"
PYPKG_SETUPTOOLS_SHA256="8199222558df7c86216af4f84c30e9b34a61d8ba19366cc914424cdbd28252f6"

# ---- native: twelve mipsel extension modules come out of these six -------
# tornado's C speedup is optional and its published wheel carries an x86-64
# copy of it, which is exactly what --no-binary :all: above is for.
PYPKG_TORNADO_FILE="tornado-6.4.tar.gz"
PYPKG_TORNADO_SHA256="72291fa6e6bc84e626589f1c29d90a5a6d593ef5ae68052ee2ef000dfd273dee"
# markupsafe -- jinja2's escape accelerator, on the path of every gcode macro
# klippy renders. A pure-python fallback (_native.py) exists if it ever fights.
PYPKG_MARKUPSAFE_FILE="MarkupSafe-2.1.5.tar.gz"
PYPKG_MARKUPSAFE_SHA256="d283d37a890ba4c1ae73ffadf8046435c76e7bc2247bbb63c00bd1a709c6544b"
# cffi -- klippy's chelper, and the package that justifies the whole glibc
# decision: this is what dlopens FlashForge's own c_helper.so, measured. Wants
# libffi, and gets the static -fPIC one patch.sh already built for _ctypes.
PYPKG_CFFI_FILE="cffi-1.17.1.tar.gz"
PYPKG_CFFI_SHA256="1c39c6016c32bc48dd54561950ebd6836e1670f2ae46128f67cf49e789c52824"
# greenlet -- klippy's reactor. The MIPS stack-switching assembly was never
# the problem; C++ designated initializers were. greenlet 3.x writes its
# PyTypeObjects as designated initializers, which gcc 7.2 -- the only compiler
# that produces this printer's ABI -- accepts ONLY in declaration order with
# no gaps, and refuses anything else with "sorry, unimplemented: non-trivial
# designated initializers not supported". patch.sh runs
# tools/python-packages/fill-designators.py over the sources first. Bumping
# this pin means re-running that, and it fails loudly rather than quietly: the
# script refuses to guess when a designator is not in the target's own headers.
PYPKG_GREENLET_FILE="greenlet-3.1.1.tar.gz"
PYPKG_GREENLET_SHA256="4ce3ac6cdb6adf7946475d7ef31777c26d94bccc377e070a7986bd2d5c515467"
# lmdb -- Moonraker's database AT THE PIN ABOVE, and the package that
# disappears the moment MOONRAKER_VERSION moves past 80c7620 (lmdb -> sqlite),
# which is the whole reason for having built an interpreter with _sqlite3.
# patch.sh sets LMDB_FORCE_CPYTHON=1; do not "fix" that with LMDB_FORCE_CFFI=0
# -- setup.py tests the variable for PRESENCE, so the string "0" selects cffi.
PYPKG_LMDB_FILE="lmdb-1.4.1.tar.gz"
PYPKG_LMDB_SHA256="1f4c76af24e907593487c904ef5eba1993beb38ed385af82adb25a858f2d658d"
# streaming-form-data -- Moonraker's upload parser, Cython.
#
# NOT 1.13.0, which is what moonraker-requirements.txt pins. That release
# ships a PREGENERATED _parser.c from a Cython that predates 3.13: it calls
# the four-argument _PyLong_AsByteArray, which grew a fifth parameter in
# 3.13, so every integer conversion in the file fails to compile. 1.19.1
# ships C that is already regenerated, which is also why no Cython is pinned
# among the host backends below. Moonraker asks five names of it
# (StreamingFormDataParser, ParseFailedException, FileTarget, ValueTarget,
# SHA256Target), all present throughout the 1.x line -- and this pin is why
# smart_open above is in the list at all.
PYPKG_STREAMING_FORM_DATA_FILE="streaming_form_data-1.19.1.tar.gz"
PYPKG_STREAMING_FORM_DATA_SHA256="8172dd509a42ac0d1033deb1d13acb68f0cbae7e1492257d13af0922bdc4dd9b"
# pillow -- Moonraker's ONLY use of it is components/file_manager/metadata.py,
# which runs as a SUBPROCESS to pull thumbnails out of gcode, so it is not on
# the startup path: without it Moonraker runs and Mainsail shows no thumbnails.
#
# NOT 10.3.0, which is what moonraker-requirements.txt pins, and not for any
# reason to do with this target: 10.3.0 CANNOT BE BUILT BY ANY 3.13, cross or
# native. Its setup.py reads its own version with exec(...) followed by
# locals()["__version__"], and PEP 667 (new in 3.13) made a function's
# locals() a snapshot -- so the name exec() defined is not there any more and
# setup.py dies with KeyError: '__version__' before it compiles a single file.
# 11.0.0 is the first line that supports 3.13. Moonraker asks PIL for
# Image.open, resize and save, all unchanged across that gap.
PYPKG_PILLOW_FILE="pillow-11.0.0.tar.gz"
PYPKG_PILLOW_SHA256="72bacbaf24ac003fea9bff9837d1eedb6088758d41e100c1552930151f677739"

# ---- host-side build backends. THESE NEVER SHIP. -------------------------
# Two of the sdists above name a PEP 517 backend that is not setuptools, and
# bin/patch.sh builds everything with --no-build-isolation -- which is not a
# preference either: with isolation ON, pip rewrites PYTHONPATH for the build
# subprocess, the cross _sysconfigdata module stops being importable, and the
# build either dies outright ("No module named
# '_sysconfigdata__linux_mipsel-linux-gnu'") or, far worse, silently answers
# every sysconfig question for x86-64 and produces host objects. So every
# backend has to be present in advance, and every one is pinned by hash for
# the same reason the compilers are: this is code that runs on the build
# machine and decides what the printer executes.
#
# Both bootstrap themselves (their sdists declare backend-path), so no third
# package is needed to install them, and neither is on PYPKG_LIST -- they go
# into the throwaway x86-64 build-python and are thrown away with it.
# setuptools, the third backend, is on the ship list above.
PYPKG_HOST_LIST="flit-core poetry-core"
PYPKG_FLIT_CORE_FILE="flit_core-3.10.1.tar.gz"         # jinja2's backend
PYPKG_FLIT_CORE_SHA256="66e5b87874a0d6e39691f0e22f09306736b633548670ad3c09ec9db03c5662f7"
PYPKG_POETRY_CORE_FILE="poetry_core-1.9.1.tar.gz"      # libnacl's backend
PYPKG_POETRY_CORE_SHA256="7a2d49214bf58b4f17f99d6891d947a9836c9899a67a5069f52d7b67217f61b8"

# --------------------------------------------------------------- libsodium
# The LAST /usr/prog string in the picture. libnacl -- and with it Moonraker's
# `authorization` component, which signs its JWTs with ed25519 -- is pure
# python and loads libsodium through ctypes.cdll.LoadLibrary, i.e. dlopen. So
# unlike the interpreter's seven static dependencies this one MUST be shared,
# and it is the only library of ours that lands in $MODDIR/lib as a .so.
#
# It costs anvil-env.sh nothing, which is the pleasant part and worth writing
# down because it is an accident: libnacl's third fallback is
# __file__[0:__file__.find("lib")+3] + "/libsodium.so", and __file__ is
# $MODDIR/lib/python3.13/site-packages/libnacl/__init__.py -- so it resolves
# $MODDIR/lib/libsodium.so BY ABSOLUTE PATH, measured working with
# LD_LIBRARY_PATH unset entirely. That works because the prefix happens to
# contain the string "lib"; a prefix without it would break silently. Adding
# $MODDIR/lib to ANVIL_LIBS also works and is the belt-and-braces option, but
# it would put our libraries in front of everything for every mod process,
# which is the kind of entry this file's own history argues against.
#
# 1.0.20 is the current stable release and there is nothing exotic about the
# pin. It cross-builds in 24 seconds and is cached on this version string in
# work/.sodium, the same shape as work/.s6 and work/.py313.
SODIUM_VERSION="1.0.20"
SODIUM_SHA256="ebb65ef6ca439333c2bb41a0c1990587288da07f6c7fd07cb3a18cc18d30ce19"
